    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p12_b11",
      "text": "Co-adaptation budget. We vary (C, S): the number of trainable CLIP vision\nblocks (attention) and the number of SAM blocks equipped with semantic\nadapters. Table 7 reveals an asymmetric effect: performance is more sensitive\nto CLIP adaptation than to increasing SAM semantic depth. While increasing\nthe number of trainable CLIP blocks consistently improves or stabilizes perfor-\nmance, expanding semantic adapters across SAM layers yields only marginal\ngains, suggesting that SAM requires only modest semantic capacity once strong\nsemantic guidance is available. This trend is consistent with the scaling results in\nExperiment 3, where increasing CLIP capacity (CLIP-L) provided larger gains\nthan scaling the SAM backbone (SAM-H), indicating that the semantic encoder\nplays a dominant role in the framework\u2019s performance.\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        412.88,
        480.62,
        542.4
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        1651,
        1922,
        2169
      ],
